The residual revealed an important fourth component: a coherent set of peaks at roughly 19.35°, 22.04°, 31.46°, 36.5–37.0°, 45.07°, 50.7°, and 56.85°. Their pseudocubic spacing is characteristic of an ordered perovskite-like calcium-rich tungstate (most plausibly Ca₃WO₆), not residual error from the three initially assigned phases. I’m now adapting an isostructural reference and testing it explicitly.

Adding the Ca₃WO₆ model drops Rwp from about 11.7% to below 8% before any coordinate refinement, and its fitted abundance is near 10–13 wt%. The apparent CaO scale becomes unstable or collapses when this phase is included, so I’m now testing the parsimonious three-phase inventory (CaWO₄ + WO₃ + Ca₃WO₆) with CaO omitted; this is the key phase-count decision.

The three-phase model is now strongly supported: with the physically expected low-angle surface correction and a [001] March–Dollase term on monoclinic WO₃, Rwp falls to about 4.0% (near the counting-statistical floor) while the fractions remain stable at roughly 70.6% CaWO₄, 15.9% WO₃, and 13.5% Ca₃WO₆. I’m checking the remaining texture/coordinate degrees of freedom rather than adding another phase.

The refined phase fractions have converged to about 69.6%, 16.0%, and 14.4%, very close to a simple 70:16:14 mixture. Coordinate refinement lowers Rwp further to 3.6%. I’m checking Ca-based double-perovskite analogues now because they may give a more defensible Ca₃WO₆ cation/oxygen geometry than the initial Sr₂CaWO₆ substitution, even if both index the peaks equally well.

The phase inventory is now well constrained: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and ordered double-perovskite Ca₃WO₆. I’m comparing the final analogue-derived Ca₃WO₆ refinements, then I’ll run structural sanity checks and package the reproducible GSAS-II outputs.

The Mg- and Co-parent Ca₃WO₆ models converge to the same refined structure and nearly identical fractions; their Rwp differs by only 0.0003 percentage point. I’ll use the chemically closer Ca₂MgWO₆ lineage unless the geometry checks expose a problem, since Mg→Ca is the cleaner analogue substitution.

